# Telecom Offensive Security Audit — Workpaper

**Engagement:** {{ engagement_id }}
**Run:** {{ run_id }}
**Prepared:** {{ generated_at }}

## Scope & population

This workpaper covers the AWS cyber posture surface (S3 public access, IAM external principals, and security-group ingress), its separate process ledger, selected Technology findings with independent validator receipts, and two independently collected ASM/VM views of the same estate: the **attack-surface-management (ASM) view** — assets,
externally exposed services and websites, attack-surface-rule alerts, incidents,
and the declared owned IPv4/IPv6 address space — and the **vulnerability-
management (VM) view** — scanned assets, scanner findings, per-CVE scan
observations, and the scanner run ledger — joined on IPv4 address, plus this
ASM/VM evidence-bundle, finding-candidate, rule and source-run ledgers. AWS and ASM/VM process ledgers remain separate because their sources and populations differ.

Coverage is the full loaded population, not a sample. The two views are never
merged into one "truth" table: every row states which source it came from, and
the checks are written to report the *disagreements* between the views (ASM-only
exposure, VM-only assets, cross-source duplicates, uncorroborated inferences),
because those disagreements are the audit findings and a merged table would
destroy them.

Externally inferred CVEs in the ASM feed are banner-parse inferences, not
scan-confirmed findings. Findings that rest on an inference alone say so in
`details` (`validation=live_probe_required` / `corroborated=false`) and may not
be restated in the report body as confirmed vulnerabilities.

